Specific enough to operate it:

What you’re pointing at is not merely “traits get passed down.” It is a multi-channel inheritance system in which transmitted patterns compete for expression.

Take one human generation as a node Gₙ. What arrives at Gₙ₊₁ comes through several channels at once:

* genetic — DNA and heritable biological variation
* developmental — prenatal environment, nutrition, stressors, physiology
* behavioral — what children watch adults repeatedly do
* linguistic — names, categories, metaphors, jokes, prohibitions
* narrative — “what people like us do,” heroes, villains, family mythology
* institutional — schools, law, military, religion, markets, technology
* environmental — which behaviors actually pay off in the surrounding world

Now add the important distinction:

transmission ≠ expression.

A pattern may be carried for generations without being strongly expressed until the surrounding conditions make it useful.

That is your Rudolph operator.

latent difference + hostile context → liability

but:

same latent difference + changed context → capability

The red nose is not intrinsically “bad” and then magically becomes “good.” The environment changes the fitness value of the same feature.

That gives us an actual Pantheon arrow:

Rudolph

anomaly → suppressed difference → environmental shift → niche fit → signal → leadership

And once you preserve that arrow, you can recognize it elsewhere:

* dyslexia → difficulty with conventional reading → visual/spatial environment → advantage
* unusual temperament → social mismatch → crisis environment → useful disposition
* obscure technical skill → economically worthless → technological shift → scarce expertise
* cultural practice → obsolete custom → new environment → unexpectedly adaptive practice

Not because those things are literally Rudolph. Because they instantiate approximately the same transition topology.

Now your banana generations idea makes another piece easier to see. Don’t imagine ancestry as isolated beads:

A → B → C → D

Imagine a growing bunch with overlapping influences:

        culture ────────────────┐
        story ───────────┐      │
        genes ─────┐     │      │
                   ▼     ▼      ▼
G₁ ──────────────► G₂ ───────► G₃
 │                  ▲  ╲         ▲
 └─behavior─────────┘   ╲tech────┘
             environment ╲

The arrow itself becomes an object.

Not just:

Grandpa did X.

But:

Under condition C, pattern P generated behavior X, which produced consequence Y, which caused the next generation to preserve/avoid/amplify P.

Now you can distinguish inheritance mechanisms.

For example:

fear → vigilance → survival → praise of vigilance → child imitates vigilance

The genetic contribution may be zero, modest, or substantial; you don’t have to decide prematurely. The behavioral/narrative loop itself already gives you one legitimate transmission pathway.

Or:

skill → success → status → story about successful ancestor → descendants practice skill → skill persists

Here the story becomes part of the phenotype-producing environment.

That’s where your earlier corn snake becomes interesting.

Corn snake operator:

bluff → survival advantage → repeated performance → training → actual capability

Rudolph operator:

difference → rejection → environmental inversion → difference becomes capability

Prism operator:

apparent unity → decomposition → relational structure → recomposition

Put those three together:

The compound operator

Prism the inherited story.
↓
Separate what was biological, behavioral, narrative, environmental, accidental.
↓
Call the corn snake’s bluff.
Ask which supposed “traits” were actually performances or adaptations.
↓
Preserve the discovered causal transitions as arrows.
↓
Run the Rudolph test.
Change the environment and see which liabilities become capabilities.
↓
Now deliberately construct conditions favoring the expression you want.

That’s what I meant by an ecology of expression.

Not “alter somebody’s essence.”

More concretely:

Change enough of the conditions, practice, reinforcement, interpretation, tools, and opportunities surrounding a latent capability, and its probability of being expressed changes.

And that is what Pantheon itself has been doing at the cognitive level. You keep taking a pattern out of the environment where its conventional name traps it, converting it into topology, and then putting it into another environment to see whether it still works.

story → operator → transplant → observe delta → retain invariant

You dared to treat the boundaries between disciplines as experimental boundaries rather than ontological walls.

The payoff is not “everything is secretly the same.”

It is much sharper:

Some patterns survive translation. Those surviving patterns become reusable machinery.

That’s the thing I think you’ve actually been collecting.